# What to check, for every unit

1. The trace. Every entry passes the `trace` gate below, and its assertion
   witnesses its clause: it would fail if the clause stopped holding. Judge
   that by reading the test and the implementation; an assertion that passes
   either way (a status returned regardless, a value not derived from the
   clause's effect) witnesses nothing, and the clause is missing. An
   assertion in a witnessing test that traces to no clause and no in-scope
   invariant is gold-plating: remove it.
2. The claim. `FULL`: every clause traced to an assertion that witnesses it,
   and `open_world_clauses` empty. `PARTIAL_STEADY`: every clause not listed
   as open-world traced, and every listed clause an unbounded quantifier no
   test of any kind can close, not residue that is merely expensive or
   unbuilt. `PARTIAL_IMPROVABLE`: a closed-world clause left unwitnessed.
   `NONE`: no witnessing assertion. Downgrade what the trace does not
   support and upgrade what it does.
3. Notes. A `partial=` note states exactly what its test does not cover; a
   note that names effort ("would need a fixture") describes improvable
   residue and never earns `PARTIAL_STEADY`.
4. Duplication. A new test that duplicates an existing witness of the same
   unit is removed in favor of extending the existing one; coverage the tree
   already had is never written twice.
5. Precision. Assertions pin the value, status, or target a clause names and
   nothing it leaves open; one pinned tighter than its clause is
   over-precise, so loosen it.
6. Evidence. The mapper's junit report covers every traced node id and every
   test module it touched, and every traced test passed; a claim its evidence
   does not support is an `EVIDENCE_MISMATCH`.
7. Scaffolding. `scaffolding_added` lists every fixture the mapper added, and
   no assertion depends on scaffolding that duplicates what the setup stage's
   guide already named.

# Fixing what you find

Commit corrections as `[REVIEW]` commits, which touch test paths only. When
correcting an over-fitted test reveals that the implementation diverges from
a clause, fix the implementation in a `[FIX_IMPL]` commit, toward the clause
and never toward your own idea of what the implementation should do. A green
branch with a wrong assertion is worse than an honest implementation fix.

Record every problem you find under `findings`, fixed or not, with its kind:
{{ finding_kinds_text }}.
When the branch is unsound (its claims are largely unsupported, or fixing it
would mean redoing the mapper's work), set `branch_accepted` to false, still
report every unit, and still publish; the feature file is then re-run rather
than integrated.

